OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026", "title": "A strict four-row permanent inequality and permutation moments", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/main.pdf", "family": "238", "family_title": "Optimal logarithmic mixing of the Thorp shuffle", "abstract_html": "We prove a permanent inequality with exponent strictly below two for laws on <i>S</i><sub>4</sub> sufficiently close to uniform and with exactly uniform coordinate marginals. Applied to a four-row recursion, it shows that an absolute number of coordinate sweeps brings the full permutation of the Thorp shuffle on \\(N=2^d\\) cards to total-variation distance tending to zero from uniform as \\(N\\to\\infty\\). Thus the mixing time has the optimal order \\(O(\\log N)\\) in physical shuffles.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (A robust four-row inequality)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a function \\(f:\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\to[0,\\infty)\\), define \\( \\|f\\|_p=\\left(\\frac14\\sum_{j=1}^4 f(j)^p\\right)^{1/p}. \\) Let \\(u\\) be uniform measure on \\(S_4\\), and use the convention …", "statement": "<p>For a function \\(f:\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\to[0,\\infty)\\), define\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_p=\\left(\\frac14\\sum_{j=1}^4 f(j)^p\\right)^{1/p}.\n\\]\nLet \\(u\\) be uniform measure on \\(S_4\\), and use the convention\n\\[\n\\|\\nu-u\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n=\\frac12\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_4}|\\nu(\\pi)-u(\\pi)|.\n\\]</p><p>There are absolute constants \\(p_0\\in(4/3,2)\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) such that, whenever a probability measure \\(\\nu\\) on \\(S_4\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\nu-u\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}&lt;\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\nu\\{\\pi:\\pi(i)=j\\}=\\frac14\n\\quad(1\\le i,j\\le4),\n\\]\nevery four nonnegative functions \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_4\\) on \\(\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\nu\\prod_{i=1}^4 f_i(\\pi(i))\n\\le\n\\prod_{i=1}^4\\|f_i\\|_{p_0}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A zero function makes the assertion immediate. By homogeneity, normalize each nonzero function to have \\(\\|f_i\\|_2=1\\). The resulting space of quadruples is compact.</p><p>The exponent-two Carlen–Lieb–Loss inequality is\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_u\\prod_i f_i(\\pi(i))\n\\le\\prod_i\\|f_i\\|_2.\n\\]\nIts normalization follows directly from\n\\[\n\\operatorname{perm}(f_i(j))\n\\le\n\\frac{4!}{4^2}\n\\prod_i\\left(\\sum_j f_i(j)^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe equality classification for nonzero nonnegative columns gives exactly one exceptional quadruple on the normalized compact space:\n\\[\nf_i\\equiv1\n\\qquad(1\\le i\\le4).\n\\]\nThese are the bound and equality statement of Carlen–Lieb–Loss [5, Theorem 1.1 and equation (1.4)].</p><p>Fix \\(p_*\\in(4/3,2)\\). Near the exceptional quadruple, the means are positive, so renormalize by the means and write\n\\[\nf_i=1+g_i,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_jg_i(j)=0.\n\\]\nProposition 1 proves the desired inequality for every \\(p\\in[p_*,2]\\) in a fixed neighborhood of the constant quadruple, provided the law has exact uniform marginals and is sufficiently close to \\(u\\).</p><p>Return to the \\(L^2\\)-normalized compact space. The condition just obtained contains a fixed open neighborhood \\(U\\) of its constant quadruple. On the compact complement of \\(U\\), the exponent-two inequality has a positive minimum gap. Both sides are continuous in the functions; they are also continuous in the exponent and in the probability masses of \\(\\nu\\).</p><p>Choose \\(p_0\\in[p_*,2)\\) sufficiently close to two, then \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon\\le\\varepsilon_*\\) sufficiently small, so that this gap remains positive on the complement of \\(U\\). Proposition 1 applies on \\(U\\) with these same constants. These two regions cover all normalized quadruples, proving the theorem.</p><p>The exact marginal assumption is structural. If\n\\[\na(j)=\\nu\\{\\pi:\\pi(i)=j\\}-\\frac14\n\\]\nis nonzero for some \\(i\\), choose \\(g=a\\), set \\(f_i=1+tg\\), and set the other functions equal to one. The derivative at \\(t=0\\) of the left side minus the right side of (1) is\n\\[\n\\sum_j a(j)^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nSmall positive \\(t\\) therefore contradicts (1).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2 (A fixed moment with a degree saving)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(N=2^d\\), with \\(d\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(N=2^d\\), with \\(d\\ge1\\). Positions are binary vectors in \\(\\{0,1\\}^d\\). One coordinate sweep visits the coordinate directions in the fixed order \\(1,\\ldots,d\\); in each direction, independently on every edge, it swaps the two cards with probability \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mu_N\\) be the law of one sweep. For a partition \\(\\lambda\\vdash N\\), let \\(\\rho_\\lambda\\) be the corresponding irreducible unitary representation of \\(S_N\\), let \\(D_\\lambda\\) be its dimension, and let \\(\\lambda'\\) be its conjugate partition. Define\n\\[\nT_N(\\lambda)=\\sum_{g\\in S_N}\\mu_N(g)\\rho_\\lambda(g),\n\\qquad\nB_N(\\lambda)=T_N(\\lambda)T_N(\\lambda)^*.\n\\]\nAll traces are ordinary, unnormalized matrix traces. Unmarked logarithms are natural.</p><p>There exist an even integer \\(r\\ge2\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), independent of \\(N\\), such that\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\operatorname{Tr}B_N(\\lambda)^r\n\\le D_\\lambda^{\\,1-\\eta}\n\\qquad(\\lambda\\vdash N).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]</p><p>Consequently, there is an absolute integer \\(q\\) such that the total-variation distance of \\(q\\) independent forward sweeps from uniform measure on \\(S_N\\) tends to zero as \\(N\\to\\infty\\), uniformly over the starting deck.</p><p>A physical Thorp shuffle consists of independent fair switches in one binary-coordinate direction followed by cyclic rotation of the coordinates. Thus the mixing time has optimal order \\(O(\\log N)\\) in physical shuffles.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the constants in their logical order.</p><p>Theorem 1 first gives \\(p_0\\) and\n\\[\n\\theta=1-\\frac1{p_0}&lt;\\frac12.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) so small that\n\\[\nb:=\\frac{\\theta}{1/2-\\delta}&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe universal degree constant \\(c\\) in Proposition 9 is independent of this choice.</p><p>Choose \\(H\\) sufficiently large and \\(\\epsilon\\) sufficiently small that\n\\[\n\\epsilon+\\frac{4b}{H}&lt;\\frac{(1-b)c}{4}.\n\\]\nThen choose the cutoff \\(M\\) required by Proposition 8, increasing it further so that the errors in Proposition 9 satisfy\n\\[\nE_{N,K}&lt;\\frac{(1-b)cK}{4b}\n\\qquad(N&gt;M).\n\\]\nThis is possible uniformly: the first error divided by \\(K\\) is \\(O(1/N)\\), and the second is \\(O(\\log N/\\sqrt N)\\) in its stated range.</p><p>Only now choose the even moment \\(r\\). It must:</p><p class=\"li\">make the four-site column law admissible for Theorem 1, as required in Proposition 4;</p><p class=\"li\">enforce the sparse stopping rule of Proposition 7;</p><p class=\"li\">make \\(F_r(\\alpha)\\le1\\) for all nontrivial shapes of all dyadic sizes at most \\(M\\).</p><p>These are finitely many or fixed-threshold requirements, by Propositions 3 and 7; all persist on increasing \\(r\\).</p><p>For an expanded root \\(\\lambda\\vdash N\\), put\n\\[\nK=k(\\lambda)&gt;0,\n\\]\nand orient \\(\\lambda\\) so that its first row is a longest side. Propositions 8 and 9 give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log F_r(\\lambda)\n&amp;\\le\n\\epsilon K+\nb\\left\\{R_H(\\lambda)+\\frac K2\\log(N/K)\\right\\}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\nb\\log D_\\lambda+\n\\epsilon K+\\frac{4bK}{H}+bE_{N,K}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac{1+b}{2}\\log D_\\lambda.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIn the second line, the additional negative term\n\\[\n-\\frac{bK}{2}\\log(N/K)\n\\]\nhas been discarded.</p><p>Thus\n\\[\n\\eta=\\frac{1-b}{2}&gt;0\n\\]\nworks at every expanded root:\n\\[\nF_r(\\lambda)\\le D_\\lambda^{\\,1-\\eta}.\n\\]\nAt a stopped root,\n\\[\nF_r(\\lambda)\\le1\\le D_\\lambda^{\\,1-\\eta}.\n\\]\nThe row representation has \\(F_r=1\\), while the column representation is killed by Proposition 3. This proves the moment assertion for every dyadic \\(N\\).</p><p>Proposition 10 now supplies an absolute integer \\(q\\) for which \\(q\\) independent forward sweeps converge to uniform in total variation, uniformly over starting decks.</p><p>To check physical time, let\n\\[\nR(x_1,\\ldots,x_d)=(x_2,\\ldots,x_d,x_1),\n\\]\nand let \\(S_t\\) be the switches in the first coordinate. A physical step is \\(RS_t\\). If \\(Y_t\\) is the accumulated permutation and\n\\[\nX_t=R^{-t}Y_t,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nX_{t+1}=(R^{-t}S_{t+1}R^t)X_t.\n\\]\nThe conjugated directions visit all coordinates in a fixed cyclic order, and \\(R^d=I\\). Relabeling that order as \\(1,\\ldots,d\\) gives exactly one sweep after \\(d\\) physical shuffles. Hence the upper bound is \\(O(d)=O(\\log N)\\).</p><p>For the opposite order, after \\(t\\) physical shuffles there are only \\(2^{tN/2}\\) coin strings. If \\(\\mu_t\\) is the resulting law, then\n\\[\n\\|\\mu_t-U_{S_N}\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\ge\n1-\\frac{2^{tN/2}}{N!}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nIn particular, distance at most \\(1/4\\) requires\n\\[\nt\\ge\n\\left\\lceil\n\\frac2N\\log_2\\frac{3N!}{4}\n\\right\\rceil\n=\n2d-O(1).\n\\]\nThis supplies the matching lower order and completes the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Local robust inequality near the constant functions.** Fix \\(p_*\\in(4/3,2)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Local robust inequality near the constant functions.**</p><p>Fix \\(p_*\\in(4/3,2)\\). Scalar expectations and inner products on four points use uniform measure:\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_j h(j)=\\frac14\\sum_{j=1}^4h(j),\n\\qquad\n\\langle g,h\\rangle=\\frac14\\sum_{j=1}^4g(j)h(j).\n\\]</p><p>There exist \\(b_*&gt;0\\) and \\(\\varepsilon_*&gt;0\\) with the following property. Let \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_4\\) be real-valued functions on four points satisfying\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_jg_i(j)=0,\n\\qquad\nb:=\\max_i\\|g_i\\|_\\infty&lt;b_*,\n\\]\nand let \\(\\nu\\) be a probability measure on \\(S_4\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\|\\nu-u\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\le\\varepsilon_*,\n\\qquad\n\\nu\\{\\pi:\\pi(i)=j\\}=\\frac14.\n\\]\nThen, for every \\(p\\in[p_*,2]\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\nu\\prod_{i=1}^4(1+g_i(\\pi(i)))\n\\le\n\\prod_{i=1}^4\\|1+g_i\\|_p.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nV=\\sum_i\\|g_i\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(i\\ne j\\), uniform sampling without replacement gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_u g_i(\\pi(i))g_j(\\pi(j))\n=-\\frac13\\langle g_i,g_j\\rangle.\n\\]\nConsequently, the total quadratic term is\n\\[\n\\sum_{i&lt;j}\\mathbb E_u g_i(\\pi(i))g_j(\\pi(j))\n=\n\\frac16\\left(\nV-\\left\\|\\sum_i g_i\\right\\|_2^2\n\\right)\n\\le\\frac V6.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]</p><p>For a law \\(\\nu\\) at total-variation distance at most \\(\\varepsilon\\) from \\(u\\), the change in each quadratic expectation is at most\n\\[\n2\\varepsilon\\|g_i\\|_\\infty\\|g_j\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nOn four points,\n\\[\n\\|g_i\\|_\\infty\\le2\\|g_i\\|_2,\n\\]\nso the total change is at most an absolute constant times \\(\\varepsilon V\\).</p><p>All linear terms are zero under \\(\\nu\\), because every coordinate marginal is uniform. Each cubic or quartic term is at most an absolute constant times \\(bV\\) when \\(b\\le1/2\\). Indeed, bound all but two factors by \\(b\\) and apply Cauchy–Schwarz to the remaining factors; their squared expectations use only the uniform marginals. Thus\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\nu\\prod_i(1+g_i(\\pi(i)))\n\\le\n1+\\left(\\frac16+C\\varepsilon+Cb\\right)V.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]</p><p>Taylor’s formula on \\([1/2,3/2]\\), uniformly for \\(p\\in[p_*,2]\\), gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_j(1+g_i(j))^p\n=\n1+\\frac{p(p-1)}2\\|g_i\\|_2^2\n+O\\!\\left(b\\|g_i\\|_2^2\\right).\n\\]\nTaking \\(p\\)th roots and multiplying the four factors gives\n\\[\n\\prod_i\\|1+g_i\\|_p\n=\n1+\\frac{p-1}{2}V+O(bV).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]</p><p>All constants are uniform on the fixed interval of exponents. Since\n\\[\n\\frac{p_*-1}{2}&gt;\\frac16,\n\\]\nfirst choose \\(b_*&gt;0\\) and \\(\\varepsilon_*&gt;0\\) small enough that the error terms in (5) and (6) fit inside this strict gap. The desired inequality follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Tensorized four-row trace estimate.** Fix the constants \\(p_0,\\varepsilon\\) supplied by Theorem 1, and put \\( \\theta=1-\\frac1{p_0}<\\frac12. \\) Let \\(m\\ge1\\), put \\(s=4^m\\), and let \\(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_{2m}\\) be …", "statement": "<p>**Tensorized four-row trace estimate.**</p><p>Fix the constants \\(p_0,\\varepsilon\\) supplied by Theorem 1, and put\n\\[\n\\theta=1-\\frac1{p_0}&lt;\\frac12.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(m\\ge1\\), put \\(s=4^m\\), and let\n\\(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_{2m}\\) be independent random permutations in \\(S_4\\). Assume that each of their laws has total-variation distance less than \\(\\varepsilon\\) from \\(u\\) and has exactly uniform coordinate marginals.</p><p>For \\(i=1,\\ldots,4\\), define\n\\[\nx_i=(\\pi_1(i),\\ldots,\\pi_m(i)),\n\\qquad\ny_i=(\\pi_{m+1}(i),\\ldots,\\pi_{2m}(i)).\n\\]\nLet \\(U_i\\) be a positive semidefinite stochastic matrix on\n\\(\\{1,2,3,4\\}^m\\), with nonnegative entries.</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\prod_{i=1}^4U_i(x_i,y_i)\n\\le\n\\prod_{i=1}^4\n\\left(\ns^{-2}\\sum_{x,y}U_i(x,y)^{p_0}\n\\right)^{1/p_0}\n\\le\ns^{-4}\\prod_{i=1}^4(\\operatorname{Tr}U_i)^\\theta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 2.1 with \\(b=2m\\), identifying a pair of strings \\((x,y)\\) with a string of length \\(2m\\), and take the four functions to be \\(U_i\\). Since\n\\[\n4^{-2m}=s^{-2},\n\\]\nthis gives the first inequality.</p><p>Apply Lemma 2.2 to each \\(U_i\\). Each contributes a factor \\(s^{-1}\\) and a trace power\n\\(\\theta=1-1/p_0\\), yielding the second inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1 (Tensorization)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_b\\) be independent random permutations, each with a law satisfying Theorem 1 for the same exponent \\(p_0\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_b\\) be independent random permutations, each with a law satisfying Theorem 1 for the same exponent \\(p_0\\). If \\(F_i\\) is a nonnegative function on \\(\\{1,2,3,4\\}^b\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\prod_{i=1}^4\nF_i(\\pi_1(i),\\ldots,\\pi_b(i))\n\\le\n\\prod_{i=1}^4\n\\left(\n4^{-b}\n\\sum_{x\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}^b}F_i(x)^{p_0}\n\\right)^{1/p_0}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on \\(b\\). Fix the first \\(b-1\\) coordinates and apply Theorem 1 to the last coordinate. The four resulting functions on \\(b-1\\) coordinates are\n\\[\nG_i(x)=\n\\left(\n\\frac14\\sum_{j=1}^4F_i(x,j)^{p_0}\n\\right)^{1/p_0}.\n\\]\nThe induction hypothesis applies to their product, and its \\(p_0\\)th-power sums are precisely the full product-measure sums in the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Stochastic-matrix interpolation, paper equation (7).** Let \\(U\\) be a positive semidefinite stochastic matrix of order \\(s\\), with nonnegative entries.", "statement": "<p>**Stochastic-matrix interpolation, paper equation (7).**</p><p>Let \\(U\\) be a positive semidefinite stochastic matrix of order \\(s\\), with nonnegative entries. For the exponent \\(p_0\\in(4/3,2)\\) fixed in Proposition 2,\n\\[\n\\sum_{x,y}U(x,y)^{p_0}\n\\le\ns^{2-p_0}(\\operatorname{Tr}U)^{p_0-1}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\left(\ns^{-2}\\sum_{x,y}U(x,y)^{p_0}\n\\right)^{1/p_0}\n\\le\ns^{-1}(\\operatorname{Tr}U)^{1-1/p_0}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The eigenvalues of \\(U\\) lie in \\([0,1]\\). Hölder’s inequality, applied entry by entry with powers \\(2-p_0\\) and \\(p_0-1\\), gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{x,y}U(x,y)^{p_0}\n&amp;\\le\n\\left(\\sum_{x,y}U(x,y)\\right)^{2-p_0}\n\\left(\\sum_{x,y}U(x,y)^2\\right)^{p_0-1}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\ns^{2-p_0}(\\operatorname{Tr}U)^{p_0-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(7)\n\\]\nThe final inequality uses\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}U^2\\le\\operatorname{Tr}U.\n\\]\nTaking the \\(p_0\\)th root with the uniform counting normalization gives the second assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 3.1 (Annihilation and the finite-size gap)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(N=2^d\\ge2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(N=2^d\\ge2\\). A permutation sends input positions to output positions, and \\(gh\\) applies \\(h\\) first. In coordinate direction \\(i\\), let \\(\\Pi_i\\) be the average of all optional edge swaps. Thus \\(\\Pi_i\\) is an orthogonal projection in every unitary representation, and\n\\[\nT_N=\\Pi_d\\cdots\\Pi_1,\n\\qquad\nB_N=T_NT_N^*.\n\\]</p><p>Every sweep kills the sign representation and every irreducible representation indexed by a partition with more than \\(N/2\\) rows.</p><p>For each fixed dyadic \\(N\\ge2\\), the operator norm of \\(T_N(\\lambda)\\) is strictly less than one on every nontrivial irreducible representation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each layer averages a subgroup\n\\[\nH\\cong(S_2)^{N/2}.\n\\]\nThe sign character averages to zero. Young’s rule says that an irreducible with \\(H\\)-fixed vectors must dominate the partition \\((2^{N/2})\\), so it has at most \\(N/2\\) rows.</p><p>For the norm assertion, equality in a product of orthogonal projections on a unit vector would force that vector to be fixed by every layer. It would then be fixed by every coordinate-edge transposition. Edge transpositions of a connected graph generate its full symmetric group, whereas a nontrivial irreducible has no invariant vector.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Four-row moment recursion, paper equation (13).** Fix \\(p_0,\\varepsilon\\) as in Theorem 1, and set \\( \\theta=1-\\frac1{p_0}<\\frac12. \\) For an even integer \\(r\\ge2\\), define …", "statement": "<p>**Four-row moment recursion, paper equation (13).**</p><p>Fix \\(p_0,\\varepsilon\\) as in Theorem 1, and set\n\\[\n\\theta=1-\\frac1{p_0}&lt;\\frac12.\n\\]\nFor an even integer \\(r\\ge2\\), define\n\\[\nF_r(\\lambda)=D_\\lambda\\operatorname{Tr}B_{|\\lambda|}(\\lambda)^r.\n\\]</p><p>For a partition \\(\\alpha\\vdash m\\), and a tuple\n\\(\\boldsymbol\\beta=(\\beta_1,\\ldots,\\beta_4)\\) whose sizes form a weak four-part composition of \\(m\\), let\n\\[\nc^\\alpha_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\n\\]\nbe the multiplicity of\n\\(\\bigotimes_{i=1}^4V_{\\beta_i}\\) in the restriction of \\(V_\\alpha\\) to\n\\(\\prod_{i=1}^4S_{|\\beta_i|}\\). Define\n\\[\nC(\\alpha)=\\sum_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\n\\left(c^\\alpha_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\\right)^2.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nEmpty partitions have dimension one.</p><p>Let \\(N=4m\\) be dyadic, and let \\(I(\\lambda)\\) be the set of tuples\n\\(\\boldsymbol\\alpha=(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_4)\\), with each \\(\\alpha_i\\vdash m\\), occurring in\n\\(\\operatorname{Res}_{(S_m)^4}^{S_N}V_\\lambda\\).</p><p>There is an absolute even threshold such that, for every even \\(r\\) above that threshold,\n\\[\nF_r(\\lambda)^{1/2}\n\\le\n\\sum_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\in I(\\lambda)}\n\\prod_{i=1}^4\n\\left(F_r(\\alpha_i)C(\\alpha_i)^\\theta\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The first \\(d-2\\) directions of a sweep on \\(N=4m\\) positions are independent sweeps within the four rows. The final two directions act independently within the columns. Hence the decomposition (8) of Lemma 4.1 holds.</p><p>Fix a tuple \\(\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\) for which every \\(F(\\alpha_i)\\) is nonzero, and use the laws \\(u_i,u,\\omega\\) of Lemma 4.1.</p><p>Let \\(a(z)\\) be the row index of position \\(z\\), and define\n\\[\n\\eta(z)=a(c^{-1}z),\n\\qquad\n\\zeta(z)=a(bz).\n\\]\nThe condition \\(bpc\\in H\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\zeta(pz)=\\eta(z)\n\\quad\\text{for every position }z:\n\\]\nsubstitute \\(z=cw\\) in \\(a(bpcw)=a(w)\\).</p><p>In each column, either coloring uses the four colors once. Different columns are independent, and the two colorings are independent because \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are.</p><p>For row \\(i\\), define a transition matrix on all \\(4^m\\) color strings by\n\\[\nU_i(x,y)\n=\n\\mathbb P_{p_i\\sim u_i}\n\\{y(p_i z)=x(z)\\text{ for every position }z\\text{ in row }i\\}.\n\\]\nConditional on the two colorings, the four row permutations are independent. With \\(\\eta_i,\\zeta_i\\) denoting the restrictions to row \\(i\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\{bpc\\in H\\}\n=\n\\mathbb E\\prod_i U_i(\\eta_i,\\zeta_i).\n\\]</p><p>The matrices \\(U_i\\) are stochastic and symmetric, since each \\(u_i\\) is invariant under inversion. Their particular origin gives the additional positivity needed in Proposition 2. Fourier inversion writes\n\\[\n\\xi_i(g)=\\frac{D_{\\alpha_i}}{m!}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(Q\\rho_{\\alpha_i}(g^{-1})),\n\\qquad\nQ=B_m(\\alpha_i)^{r/2}\\ge0.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\xi_i\\), and then \\(|\\xi_i|^2\\), are positive-definite functions. Their Fourier transforms are positive semidefinite, so the average of \\(u_i\\) in the color-string representation is positive semidefinite as well.</p><p>The four-site law of \\(M\\) sends every site exactly uniformly to the four sites. Its adjoint does too, and the same remains true of every positive power of \\(M^*M\\). Moreover, the support of \\(M^*M\\) contains the identity and the edge transpositions of the square, which generate \\(S_4\\). Its convolution powers therefore converge to uniform.</p><p>Choose the even integer \\(r\\) sufficiently large that the law of each column of\n\\[\nY=(M^*M)^{r/2}\n\\]\nsatisfies Theorem 1. Every larger even \\(r\\) does too, once a tail threshold for convergence has been chosen.</p><p>The variables defining \\((\\eta_i,\\zeta_i)\\) consist of \\(2m\\) independent four-color column assignments. Apply Proposition 2, with \\(s=4^m\\), to obtain\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\{bpc\\in H\\}\n\\le\ns^{-4}\\prod_i(\\operatorname{Tr}U_i)^\\theta.\n\\]\nLemma 4.2 applies to the squared-coefficient laws \\(u_i\\), giving\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}U_i\\le C(\\alpha_i).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\frac{N!}{(m!)^4}\\le4^N=s^4,\n\\]\nthe exponential factors cancel. Lemma 4.1 therefore bounds the contribution of the tuple by\n\\[\n\\prod_iF(\\alpha_i)C(\\alpha_i)^\\theta\n\\]\nbefore taking the square root in (10). Summing over the tuples gives (13).</p><p>All restriction multiplicities have been retained: they are accounted for by the regular trace and by \\(C(\\alpha)\\), rather than discarded during a choice of one copy.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Positive trace comparison and reduction to an overlap probability.** Use the setting of Proposition 4, but initially let \\(r\\ge2\\) be any even integer and suppress the subscript \\(r\\) in \\(F_r\\).", "statement": "<p>**Positive trace comparison and reduction to an overlap probability.**</p><p>Use the setting of Proposition 4, but initially let \\(r\\ge2\\) be any even integer and suppress the subscript \\(r\\) in \\(F_r\\).</p><p>Use the last two coordinates as the row index of a \\(4\\)-by-\\(m\\) array. Let\n\\[\nH=(S_m)^4.\n\\]\nLet \\(M\\) be the operator of the final two coordinate layers, acting independently in the \\(m\\) columns, and put\n\\[\nA=B_m^{\\otimes4},\n\\qquad\nB_N=MAM^*,\n\\qquad\nX=A^{r/2},\n\\qquad\nY=(M^*M)^{r/2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\in I(\\lambda)\\), let \\(X_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}\\) be \\(X\\) multiplied by its central \\(H\\)-isotype projection.</p><p>Then\n\\[\nF(\\lambda)^{1/2}\n\\le\n\\sum_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\in I(\\lambda)}\n\\left(\nD_\\lambda\n\\operatorname{Tr}\n(X_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}YX_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}Y)_\\lambda\n\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]</p><p>Fix one tuple \\(\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\). Write\n\\[\nX_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}\n=\\sum_{p\\in H}\\xi(p)p,\n\\qquad\n\\xi(p)=\\prod_{i=1}^4\\xi_i(p_i).\n\\]\nIf all \\(F(\\alpha_i)\\) are nonzero, define independent row-permutation laws\n\\[\nu_i(p_i)=\n\\frac{|\\xi_i(p_i)|^2}{\\sum_{g\\in S_m}|\\xi_i(g)|^2},\n\\qquad\nu=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^4u_i.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the probability law on the column group \\((S_4)^m\\) whose convolution operator is \\(Y\\). If \\(p\\sim u\\), and \\(b,c\\) are independent with law \\(\\omega\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathrm{reg}}\n(X_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}YX_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}Y)\n\\le\n\\prod_{i=1}^4F(\\alpha_i)\n\\frac{N!}{(m!)^4}\n\\mathbb P\\{bpc\\in H\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nA tuple for which some \\(F(\\alpha_i)=0\\) contributes zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Araki–Lieb–Thirring inequality for positive operators, with exponent \\(r/2\\ge1\\) and outer power two, gives\n\\[\nF(\\lambda)\n\\le\nD_\\lambda\\operatorname{Tr}(XYXY)_\\lambda\n=\nD_\\lambda\n\\|Y^{1/2}X_\\lambda Y^{1/2}\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(MAM^*\\) and \\(A^{1/2}M^*MA^{1/2}\\) have the same nonzero eigenvalues, after which the stated positive-matrix inequality applies [1]; see also [2, Theorem 1].</p><p>The row operator \\(X=A^{r/2}\\) is a positive element of the group algebra of \\(H\\). The central isotype projection commutes with \\(X\\), so \\(X_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}\\) is positive in every unitary representation. On \\(V_\\lambda\\), it acts on all copies of that row type, not on a chosen copy. The Hilbert–Schmidt triangle inequality in (9) gives (10).</p><p>In every irreducible representation,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}\n(X_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}YX_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}Y)\n=\n\\|Y^{1/2}X_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}Y^{1/2}\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe regular representation of \\(S_N\\) contains \\(D_\\lambda\\) copies of \\(V_\\lambda\\), so its trace bounds the quantity inside the corresponding square root in (10).</p><p>Each row factor has Fourier block \\(B_m(\\alpha_i)^{r/2}\\) on \\(\\alpha_i\\) and zero blocks elsewhere. Plancherel on \\(S_m\\) gives\n\\[\nm!\\sum_{p_i}|\\xi_i(p_i)|^2\n=\nD_{\\alpha_i}\\operatorname{Tr}B_m(\\alpha_i)^r\n=\nF(\\alpha_i).\n\\]\nIf one of these quantities is zero, the tuple contributes nothing.</p><p>Because \\(r/2\\) is an integer, \\(Y=(M^*M)^{r/2}\\) is convolution by a probability law \\(\\omega\\) on the column group. Expanding the regular trace gives\n\\[\nN!\\sum_{p,q\\in H}\\sum_{b,c}\n\\xi(p)\\xi(q)\\omega(b)\\omega(c)\n\\mathbf1_{\\{pbqc=e\\}}.\n\\]\nBound each coefficient product by\n\\[\n|\\xi(p)\\xi(q)|\n\\le\\frac{|\\xi(p)|^2+|\\xi(q)|^2}{2}.\n\\]\nThe two square terms have equal sums: cyclically interchange \\((p,b)\\) and \\((q,c)\\) in the identity \\(pbqc=e\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(p,b,c\\), there is at most one possible \\(q\\), and it lies in \\(H\\) exactly when\n\\[\nb^{-1}p^{-1}c^{-1}\\in H.\n\\]\nSelf-adjointness gives\n\\[\n\\xi(p^{-1})=\\overline{\\xi(p)},\n\\]\nso \\(u\\) is invariant under inversion. The real probability coefficients of the self-adjoint \\(Y\\) have the same symmetry. We may thus invert each of the three independent variables. Using the preceding Plancherel normalizations yields (11).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.1 (Color-string trace)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\alpha\\vdash m\\), let \\(Q\\ge0\\) be a nonzero operator on \\(V_\\alpha\\), and define \\( u(g)= \\frac{D_\\alpha}{m!\\operatorname{Tr}Q^2} \\left|\\operatorname{Tr}(Q\\rho_\\alpha(g))\\right|^2 \\qquad(g\\in S_m). \\) Let \\(U\\) …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\alpha\\vdash m\\), let \\(Q\\ge0\\) be a nonzero operator on \\(V_\\alpha\\), and define\n\\[\nu(g)=\n\\frac{D_\\alpha}{m!\\operatorname{Tr}Q^2}\n\\left|\\operatorname{Tr}(Q\\rho_\\alpha(g))\\right|^2\n\\qquad(g\\in S_m).\n\\]\nLet \\(U\\) be its average action on strings of length \\(m\\) over four colors.</p><p>With the normalized character inner product\n\\[\n\\langle f,g\\rangle_{S_m}\n=\\frac1{m!}\\sum_{x\\in S_m}f(x)\\overline{g(x)},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}U\n\\le\n\\langle\\chi_\\alpha^2,4^{\\#\\mathrm{cycles}}\\rangle_{S_m}\n=\n\\sum_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\n\\left(c^\\alpha_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\\right)^2\n=\nC(\\alpha).\n\\]\nThe sum is over the tuples specified in Proposition 4.</p>", "proof": "<p>Schur orthogonality normalizes \\(u\\) to have mass one. Write\n\\[\nV_\\alpha\\otimes\\overline{V_\\alpha}\n=\n\\bigoplus_\\nu V_\\nu\\otimes\\mathbb C^{a_\\nu},\n\\]\nand let \\(P_\\nu\\) be its isotypic projection. Character orthogonality gives\n\\[\n\\sum_g u(g)\\chi_\\nu(g)\n=\n\\frac{\nD_\\alpha\\operatorname{Tr}((Q\\otimes\\overline Q)P_\\nu)\n}{\nD_\\nu\\operatorname{Tr}Q^2\n}.\n\\]\nThe two commuting positive operators \\(Q\\otimes I\\) and \\(I\\otimes\\overline Q\\) satisfy\n\\[\nQ\\otimes\\overline Q\n\\le\n\\frac12\\left(Q^2\\otimes I+I\\otimes\\overline Q^{\\,2}\\right).\n\\]\nThe partial trace of \\(P_\\nu\\) over either factor commutes with the irreducible action on the other. Its trace is \\(a_\\nu D_\\nu\\), so it equals\n\\[\n\\frac{a_\\nu D_\\nu}{D_\\alpha}I.\n\\]\nThe preceding character average is therefore at most \\(a_\\nu\\).</p><p>A permutation fixes exactly \\(4^{\\#\\mathrm{cycles}(g)}\\) color strings. This is a permutation character, so its expansion in irreducible characters has nonnegative integer coefficients. Apply the preceding inequality to that expansion. It yields the first asserted bound, since \\(a_\\nu\\) is the multiplicity of \\(\\nu\\) in \\(\\chi_\\alpha^2\\).</p><p>Finally, the color-string representation is the direct sum, over all color-class sizes \\(m_1+\\cdots+m_4=m\\), of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}_{S_{m_1}\\times\\cdots\\times S_{m_4}}^{S_m}\\mathbf1.\n\\]\nFrobenius reciprocity identifies its inner product with \\(\\chi_\\alpha^2\\) as the sum of the dimensions of the commuting algebras of the corresponding restrictions of \\(V_\\alpha\\). Those dimensions are the sums of squared multiplicities in the statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 (The cost of restriction)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a partition \\(\\alpha\\vdash m\\), define \\( k(\\alpha)=m-\\max(\\alpha_1,\\alpha'_1), \\) and, for an integer \\(h\\ge1\\), define \\( t_h(\\alpha)= |\\{(i,j)\\in\\alpha:i>h,\\ j>h\\}|. \\) Thus \\(t_h(\\alpha)\\) is the number of boxes …", "statement": "<p>For a partition \\(\\alpha\\vdash m\\), define\n\\[\nk(\\alpha)=m-\\max(\\alpha_1,\\alpha'_1),\n\\]\nand, for an integer \\(h\\ge1\\), define\n\\[\nt_h(\\alpha)=\n|\\{(i,j)\\in\\alpha:i&gt;h,\\ j&gt;h\\}|.\n\\]\nThus \\(t_h(\\alpha)\\) is the number of boxes outside the union of the first \\(h\\) rows and first \\(h\\) columns.</p><p>Let \\(C(\\alpha)\\) be the squared restriction-multiplicity sum defined in Proposition 4. There is an absolute constant \\(C\\) such that\n\\[\n\\log C(\\alpha)\n\\le\nt_h(\\alpha)\\log4\n+\nC(1+h)(1+\\sqrt{k(\\alpha)})\\log(2m)\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\alpha\\vdash m\\) and every integer \\(h\\ge1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The central estimate for a residual shape \\(\\delta\\vdash t\\) is\n\\[\n\\left(c^\\delta_{\\gamma_1,\\ldots,\\gamma_4}\\right)^2\n\\le\n\\binom{t}{|\\gamma_1|,\\ldots,|\\gamma_4|}\n\\le4^t.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nTo prove it, let the multiplicity before squaring be \\(c\\). Restriction and induction dimensions give, respectively,\n\\[\nc\\prod_iD_{\\gamma_i}\\le D_\\delta,\n\\qquad\ncD_\\delta\n\\le\n\\binom{t}{|\\gamma_1|,\\ldots,|\\gamma_4|}\n\\prod_iD_{\\gamma_i}.\n\\]\nMultiplying and cancelling the positive dimensions proves (15). It is essential here to combine one restriction estimate and one induction estimate.</p><p>Both \\(C(\\alpha)\\) and \\(t_h(\\alpha)\\) are invariant under conjugation, so orient \\(\\alpha\\) with its first row of length \\(m-k\\), where \\(k=k(\\alpha)\\). Remove its first \\(h\\) rows and then the first \\(h\\) columns of the remainder. The residual straight diagram \\(\\delta\\) has size\n\\[\nt=t_h(\\alpha).\n\\]\nDeleting a first row leaves an interlacing partition, so reversing that deletion adds a horizontal strip. Deleting a first column gives the vertical analogue.</p><p>More explicitly, let \\(\\ell\\le2h\\) be the number of nonempty removed lines, let their lengths be \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_\\ell\\), and let \\(\\xi_j\\) be the trivial character of \\(S_{a_j}\\) for a removed row and the sign character for a removed column. Then\n\\[\nW=\n\\operatorname{Ind}_{S_t\\times S_{a_1}\\times\\cdots\\times S_{a_\\ell}}^{S_m}\n(V_\\delta\\otimes\\xi_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\xi_\\ell)\n\\]\ncontains \\(V_\\alpha\\) by the Pieri rules. Thus each multiplicity in a restriction of \\(V_\\alpha\\) is bounded by the corresponding multiplicity in \\(W\\).</p><p>Use this comparison only for tuples \\(\\boldsymbol\\beta\\) that occur in the original restriction of \\(V_\\alpha\\). For those tuples, the first-row inequality in the Littlewood–Richardson rule says\n\\[\n\\alpha_1\\le\\sum_i\\beta_{i,1},\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\sum_i(|\\beta_i|-\\beta_{i,1})\\le k.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]</p><p>Every intermediate diagram in a Pieri chain ending at such a \\(\\beta_i\\) is contained in it, and has at most \\(k\\) boxes below its first row. The number of partitions of any size at most \\(m\\) satisfying that condition is at most\n\\[\nQ_m(k)=(m+1)^{1+2\\lceil\\sqrt{k}\\rceil}.\n\\]\nOne way to see this is to choose the first-row length, and encode the tail by its row lengths and column lengths along a Durfee square of side at most \\(\\sqrt{k}\\). Padding the two lists to length \\(\\lceil\\sqrt{k}\\rceil\\) gives the displayed bound. For \\(k=0\\), only the first-row length is needed.</p><p>Fix such a final tuple \\(\\boldsymbol\\beta\\), and put\n\\[\nH_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}=\\prod_{i=1}^4S_{|\\beta_i|}.\n\\]\nMackey’s formula for \\(\\operatorname{Res}_{H_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}}W\\) indexes the decomposition by allocations of the residual factor and the \\(\\ell\\) strip factors among these classes. There are at most\n\\[\n(m+1)^{4(\\ell+1)}\n\\]\nallocation matrices.</p><p>For each allocation, restrict \\(V_\\delta\\) to the four allocated subsets, then add the allocated horizontal or vertical strips within each color class by Pieri. A chain ending at \\(\\beta_i\\) consists of diagrams contained in \\(\\beta_i\\), so every one obeys the small-tail bound just counted. Choosing the residual tuple and all subsequent diagrams costs at most\n\\[\nQ_m(k)^{4(\\ell+1)}.\n\\]\nEach Pieri transition is multiplicity free, and (15) bounds the residual multiplicity by \\(4^{t/2}\\). Therefore\n\\[\nc^\\alpha_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\n\\le\n\\operatorname{mult}_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\n(\\operatorname{Res}_{H_{\\boldsymbol\\beta}}W)\n\\le\n(m+1)^{4(\\ell+1)}\nQ_m(k)^{4(\\ell+1)}\n4^{t/2}.\n\\]</p><p>There are at most \\(Q_m(k)^4\\) possible final occurring tuples. Squaring this bound and summing over them gives \\(4^t\\) times\n\\[\n\\exp\\{C(1+h)(1+\\sqrt{k})\\log(2m)\\},\n\\]\nproving (14). No tail estimate for the other constituents of \\(W\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 4.3 (Hook deficits under restriction)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_4\\) be partitions with \\( c^\\lambda_{\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_4}>0. \\) For any partition \\(\\gamma\\), use …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_4\\) be partitions with\n\\[\nc^\\lambda_{\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_4}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor any partition \\(\\gamma\\), use\n\\[\nk(\\gamma)=|\\gamma|-\\max(\\gamma_1,\\gamma'_1),\n\\qquad\nt_h(\\gamma)=|\\{(i,j)\\in\\gamma:i&gt;h,\\ j&gt;h\\}|.\n\\]</p><p>Then, for every integer \\(h\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_i t_h(\\alpha_i)\\le t_h(\\lambda),\n\\qquad\n\\sum_i k(\\alpha_i)\\le k(\\lambda).\n\\tag{17}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the assertion about \\(k\\), orient the parent with its longest side as first row. The first-row inequality gives a bound by its row deficit on the sum of the child row deficits; each child’s \\(k\\) is no larger than its row deficit. If the parent’s longest side is a column, transpose every partition, which preserves Littlewood–Richardson multiplicities.</p><p>Write \\(s_\\nu\\) for the ordinary Schur function and \\(s_\\nu(X\\mid Y)\\) for its hook-Schur specialization [14, Definition 1(a)]. For the assertion about \\(t_h\\), take \\(h\\) ordinary variables \\(X\\), \\(h\\) transpose variables \\(Y\\), and an unlimited ordinary alphabet \\(Z\\). Variables in \\(X,Y\\) have degree zero and those in \\(Z\\) have degree one.</p><p>The positive hook-Schur tableau rule uses ordinary entries weakly along rows and strictly down columns, and transpose entries strictly along rows and weakly down columns [9, Section 2]. For \\(s_\\nu(X\\mid Y)\\), order all \\(X\\) letters before all \\(Y\\) letters, so the ordinary entries occupy a subdiagram. The rule gives\n\\[\ns_\\nu(X\\mid Y)\\ne0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\nu_{h+1}\\le h.\n\\]\nFor completeness, a tableau over \\(h\\) ordinary and \\(h\\) transpose letters cannot fill an \\((h+1)\\)-by-\\((h+1)\\) square: the ordinary subdiagram has at most \\(h\\) rows, and the transpose-filled remainder has at most \\(h\\) columns in each remaining row. Conversely, fill the first \\(h\\) rows with their ordinary row letters and the remaining boxes with their transpose column letters. This realizes every diagram in that hook.</p><p>Writing \\(p_j\\) for the \\(j\\)th power sum, the supersymmetric substitution is\n\\[\np_j\\longmapsto p_j(X)+(-1)^{j-1}p_j(Y).\n\\]\nApplied to the ordinary Schur coproduct, it gives\n\\[\ns_\\lambda(X+Z\\mid Y)\n=\n\\sum_{\\nu\\subseteq\\lambda}\ns_\\nu(X\\mid Y)s_{\\lambda/\\nu}(Z).\n\\]\nIts coefficients are nonnegative. The largest subdiagram of \\(\\lambda\\) in the \\((h,h)\\) hook is the union of its first \\(h\\) rows and first \\(h\\) columns. The smallest degree in \\(Z\\) is therefore exactly \\(t_h(\\lambda)\\); the corresponding skew Schur function is nonzero on an unlimited alphabet.</p><p>Apply this substitution to\n\\[\n\\prod_i s_{\\alpha_i}\n=\n\\sum_\\lambda c^\\lambda_{\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_4}s_\\lambda.\n\\]\nThe product on the left has minimum \\(Z\\)-degree\n\\[\n\\sum_i t_h(\\alpha_i).\n\\]\nEvery summand on the right has nonnegative coefficients, so an occurring parent’s minimum degree cannot be smaller. This proves the assertion about \\(t_h\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 5.1 (Sparse stopping)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(n=2^d\\), let \\(T=T_n\\), and let \\( B=TT^*, \\qquad B_\\lambda=B_n(\\lambda). \\) Empty diagrams have dimension one.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(n=2^d\\), let \\(T=T_n\\), and let\n\\[\nB=TT^*,\n\\qquad\nB_\\lambda=B_n(\\lambda).\n\\]\nEmpty diagrams have dimension one. Define\n\\[\nk(\\lambda)=n-\\max(\\lambda_1,\\lambda'_1).\n\\]</p><p>There is an absolute \\(N_0\\) such that, if \\(n\\ge N_0\\) and\n\\[\n1\\le k\\le n^{1/100},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mu\\vdash k}\nD_\\mu\\operatorname{Tr}B_{(n-k,\\mu)}\n\\le n^{-3k/10}.\n\\]</p><p>Consequently, for \\(n\\ge N_0\\), if \\(k(\\lambda)\\le n^{1/100}\\), then\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\operatorname{Tr}B_\\lambda^r\\le1\n\\qquad(r\\ge4).\n\\]</p><p>There is a fixed even \\(r_0\\ge4\\) for which the latter assertion holds for every power of two \\(n\\ge2\\) and every such \\(\\lambda\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 7.1–7.3 give\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mu\\vdash k}\nD_\\mu\\operatorname{Tr}B_{(n-k,\\mu)}\n=b_k\\le n^{-3k/10}\n\\]\nfor an absolute sufficiently large \\(N_0\\).</p><p>Suppose first that\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=n-k,\n\\qquad\n1\\le k\\le n^{1/100}.\n\\]\nIts term in \\(b_k\\) has coefficient \\(D_\\mu\\ge1\\), so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}B_\\lambda\\le n^{-3k/10}.\n\\]\nThe eigenvalues are nonnegative, and consequently\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}B_\\lambda^r\n\\le\n(\\operatorname{Tr}B_\\lambda)^r.\n\\]\nChoosing the labels of the \\(k\\) cells below the first row and then their order gives\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\le\\binom nk k!\\le n^k.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\operatorname{Tr}B_\\lambda^r\n\\le\nn^{(1-3r/10)k}\\le1\n\\qquad(r\\ge4).\n\\]</p><p>If instead\n\\[\n\\lambda'_1=n-k&gt;n/2,\n\\]\nevery matching projection vanishes on \\(V_\\lambda\\). Indeed, its fixed-space dimension is the multiplicity of \\(V_\\lambda\\) in\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}_{(S_2)^{n/2}}^{S_n}\\mathbf1.\n\\]\nRepeated horizontal Pieri shows that every constituent is obtained by adding \\(n/2\\) horizontal strips of size two. Each strip adds at most one cell to the first column, so every constituent has first-column height at most \\(n/2\\). Thus \\(B_\\lambda=0\\) in this case.</p><p>For \\(k=0\\), the trivial representation has the claimed value one and the sign representation is annihilated by a matching projection.</p><p>Finally, Proposition 3 gives operator norm strictly less than one on every nontrivial irreducible at each fixed \\(n\\). There are only finitely many powers of two below \\(N_0\\) and finitely many irreducibles at each of them. Increasing \\(r\\) to one fixed even \\(r_0\\ge4\\) therefore gives the stopping estimate also at these remaining sizes. Larger powers preserve the estimate because \\(B\\) is a positive semidefinite contraction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Branching, binomial inversion, and the endpoint trace identity.** Let \\(n=2^d\\), let \\(k\\ge1\\) satisfy \\(n\\ge2k\\), and let \\(T=T_n\\), \\(B=TT^*\\).", "statement": "<p>**Branching, binomial inversion, and the endpoint trace identity.**</p><p>Let \\(n=2^d\\), let \\(k\\ge1\\) satisfy \\(n\\ge2k\\), and let \\(T=T_n\\), \\(B=TT^*\\). For \\(0\\le s\\le k\\), let \\(\\mathcal I_s\\) be the set of ordered injections from \\([s]\\) into the \\(n\\) positions, with \\(\\mathcal I_0\\) a singleton. Let \\(T_s\\) be the induced sweep transition matrix, and define\n\\[\na_s=\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathbb C\\mathcal I_s}(TT^*)\n=\n\\sum_{x,y\\in\\mathcal I_s}T_s(x,y)^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(0\\le j\\le k\\), put\n\\[\nb_j=\\sum_{\\mu\\vdash j}D_\\mu\\operatorname{Tr}B_{(n-j,\\mu)}.\n\\]</p><p>Then\n\\[\na_s=\\sum_{j=0}^s\\binom sj b_j,\n\\qquad\nb_k=\\sum_{s=0}^k(-1)^{k-s}\\binom ks a_s\\ge0.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(s\\) tracked cards, choose a uniform initial injection and run one realized sweep. Let \\(e\\) be the number of switches used by two tracked cards. Then\n\\[\na_s=\\frac{(n)_s}{n^s}\\mathbb E\\,2^e,\n\\qquad\n(n)_s=n(n-1)\\cdots(n-s+1).\n\\tag{19}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The permutation module on \\(\\mathcal I_s\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}_{S_{n-s}}^{S_n}\\mathbf1.\n\\]\nBy repeated branching, the multiplicity of \\(V_\\lambda\\) in it is the number of standard tableaux of skew shape \\(\\lambda/(n-s)\\).</p><p>Every occurring partition has the form\n\\[\n\\lambda=(n-j,\\mu),\n\\qquad\n0\\le j\\le s,\\quad \\mu\\vdash j.\n\\]\nThe skew shape consists of the tail \\(\\mu\\) and a row of \\(s-j\\) cells whose columns exceed\n\\[\nn-s\\ge k\\ge\\mu_1.\n\\]\nThese components share neither a row nor a column. Choosing the \\(j\\) entries of the tail and then a standard tableau on it gives multiplicity\n\\[\n\\binom sjD_\\mu.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\na_s=\\sum_{j=0}^s\\binom sjb_j.\n\\]\nBinomial inversion gives (18), where positivity follows from the positive semidefiniteness of each \\(B_\\lambda\\).</p><p>For fixed initial and final positions of one card, there is exactly one possible path through the successive coordinate layers: at layer \\(i\\), its first \\(i\\) processed coordinates have their final values and its remaining coordinates still have their initial values. Thus fixed endpoints for \\(s\\) cards prescribe all their paths.</p><p>If two tracked cards use the same switch, the two prescribed coin requirements either disagree, making the endpoints impossible, or agree, saving one coin. If \\(e\\) is the number of switches shared by two tracked cards along a realized sweep, the transition probability to its realized endpoint is therefore\n\\[\n2^{-ds+e}=n^{-s}2^e.\n\\]\nAveraging first over the realized endpoint and then over a uniform initial injection gives (19).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Coupled encounter graph and exact cancellation.** Use the setting of Lemma 7.1.", "statement": "<p>**Coupled encounter graph and exact cancellation.**</p><p>Use the setting of Lemma 7.1. Choose \\(k\\) distinct uniform initial positions\n\\[\nX_1,\\ldots,X_k\n\\]\nindependently of the coins of one complete sweep.</p><p>For the realized sweep, form a graph \\(G\\) on all \\(n\\) initial positions, joining two positions if their cards share a switch. Let \\(G_X\\) be the graph induced on the selected tags. For \\(S\\subseteq[k]\\), let \\(e(S)\\) be the number of edges of \\(G_X\\) with both endpoints in \\(S\\), and define\n\\[\nw(S)=\\frac{(n)_{|S|}}{n^{|S|}}\n=\n\\prod_{i\\in S}\n\\left(\n1-\\frac{\\#\\{j\\in S:j&lt;i\\}}n\n\\right).\n\\]</p><p>A predecessor selection is a collection \\(D\\) of directed pairs \\((i,j)\\), with \\(1\\le j&lt;i\\le k\\), having at most one pair with any given first coordinate. Write \\(|D|\\) for its number of pairs and \\(\\operatorname{supp}D\\) for their endpoints. For an edge set \\(H\\), write \\(\\operatorname{supp}H\\) for its incident vertices.</p><p>Then \\(G\\) is simple and every vertex has degree \\(d\\), and\n\\[\nb_k\n=\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{S\\subseteq[k]}\n(-1)^{k-|S|}w(S)2^{e(S)}\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\nhas the exact cancellation expansion\n\\[\nb_k\n=\n\\mathbb E\n\\sum_{H\\subseteq E(G_X)}\n\\sum_{\\substack{D:\\\\\n\\operatorname{supp}H\\cup\\operatorname{supp}D=[k]}}\n(-n^{-1})^{|D|}.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Each vertex of \\(G\\) has degree \\(d\\). The graph is simple: after two cards meet at coordinate \\(i\\), their positions differ at that coordinate forever, so they cannot meet at a later coordinate.</p><p>The marginal initial positions of every subset of tags are a uniform injection. Thus (19) and (18) of Lemma 7.1 imply (20).</p><p>Expanding the product defining \\(w(S)\\) gives\n\\[\nw(S)\n=\n\\sum_{D:\\operatorname{supp}D\\subseteq S}\n(-n^{-1})^{|D|}.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n2^{e(S)}\n=\n\\sum_{H\\subseteq E(G_X[S])}1.\n\\]\nSubstitute these expansions into (20) and sum over \\(S\\). A term supported on \\(U\\subseteq[k]\\) has coefficient\n\\[\n\\sum_{S\\supseteq U}(-1)^{k-|S|},\n\\]\nwhich is zero unless \\(U=[k]\\). This yields (21).</p><p>Thus only an encounter-edge set and a predecessor selection that together cover every tag survive the inversion. Their absolute sum may now be bounded without losing that covering constraint.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Counting the covering configurations.** Let \\(n=2^d\\) be sufficiently large and let \\( 1\\le k\\le n^{1/100}. \\) Use the encounter graph and the quantities \\(b_k\\) of Lemmas 7.1–7.2.", "statement": "<p>**Counting the covering configurations.**</p><p>Let \\(n=2^d\\) be sufficiently large and let\n\\[\n1\\le k\\le n^{1/100}.\n\\]\nUse the encounter graph and the quantities \\(b_k\\) of Lemmas 7.1–7.2.</p><p>For every set of \\(v\\) vertices of \\(G\\), the number of spanned edges is at most\n\\[\n\\frac v2\\log_2v,\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\nwhere \\(0\\log_20=0\\).</p><p>If \\(F\\) is a specified forest on \\(v\\) selected tags with \\(c\\) components, then, conditional on the entire sweep,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(F\\subseteq G_X\\mid G)\n\\le\n\\frac{n^cd^{v-c}}{(n)_v}\n\\le\n2\\left(\\frac dn\\right)^{v-c}.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]</p><p>These estimates imply\n\\[\nb_k\n\\le\n2\n\\sum_{\\substack{0\\le v,c,q\\le k\\\\\n2c\\le v,\\ v+2q\\ge k}}\n\\left(4k^{5/2}\\sqrt{d/n}\\right)^v\n\\left(4k^2/n\\right)^q,\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n0\\le b_k\\le n^{-3k/10}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>To prove (22), induct on \\(d\\). Split the initial positions according to the last processed coordinate. Before the last layer, these two halves remain separate.</p><p>If the chosen set has \\(v_0,v_1\\) positions in them, induction bounds the earlier encounters by\n\\[\n\\frac12v_0\\log_2v_0+\\frac12v_1\\log_2v_1,\n\\]\nand the last matching adds at most \\(\\min(v_0,v_1)\\) edges. This is at most\n\\[\n\\frac12v\\log_2v,\n\\qquad v=v_0+v_1,\n\\]\nbecause binary entropy\n\\[\nH_2(p)=-p\\log_2p-(1-p)\\log_2(1-p)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\nH_2(p)\\ge2\\min(p,1-p)\n\\]\nby concavity on each half of \\([0,1]\\).</p><p>For (23), choose one root position per component and then successively place each child at one of at most \\(d\\) neighbors of its parent. This gives the first inequality even if some counted placements fail to be injective. For \\(v\\le k\\le n^{1/100}\\) and sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{(n)_v}{n^v}\n=\n\\prod_{i=0}^{v-1}(1-i/n)\n\\ge\n1-\\frac{v(v-1)}{2n}\n\\ge\\frac12.\n\\]\nThis gives the second inequality.</p><p>Let an encounter-edge set \\(H\\) use \\(v\\) vertices and have \\(c\\) nontrivial connected components. Then \\(2c\\le v\\), and \\(H\\) contains a spanning forest with \\(v-c\\) edges.</p><p>For fixed \\(v,c\\), there are at most \\((2k)^{2v}\\) choices for its vertex set and forest: choose the vertex set, root every component at its least vertex, and record a parent or a root marker for every vertex.</p><p>For any realization and any such forest, the number of possible edge sets extending it is at most\n\\[\n2^{e(G_X[V])}\n\\le\nv^{v/2}\n\\le\nk^{v/2}\n\\]\nby (22). A canonical choice of spanning forest for each \\(H\\), or simply overcounting all spanning forests, therefore bounds the expected number of these \\(H\\) by\n\\[\n2(2k)^{2v}k^{v/2}(d/n)^{v-c}.\n\\]\nFor \\(v=c=0\\), the empty edge set contributes one, which also obeys this bound.</p><p>There are at most\n\\[\n\\binom kq k^q\\le(2k)^{2q}\n\\]\npredecessor selections with \\(q\\) pairs. The covering condition in (21) implies\n\\[\nv+2q\\ge k.\n\\]\nSince \\(d/n\\le1\\) and \\(v-c\\ge v/2\\), taking absolute values in (21) gives (24). Impossible triples only enlarge this upper bound.</p><p>Uniformly for \\(1\\le k\\le n^{1/100}\\), sufficiently large \\(n\\) satisfies\n\\[\n4k^{5/2}\\sqrt{d/n}\\le n^{-2/5},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{2k}{\\sqrt n}\\le n^{-2/5},\n\\qquad\n2(k+1)^3\\le n^{k/10}.\n\\]\nFor example, the first left side is at most\n\\[\n4\\sqrt{\\log_2n}\\,n^{-19/40},\n\\]\nand the last left side is at most \\(16n^{3/100}\\).</p><p>Every term in (24) is therefore at most\n\\[\nn^{-2(v+2q)/5}\\le n^{-2k/5}.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\((k+1)^3\\) triples, so\n\\[\n0\\le b_k\\le n^{-3k/10}.\n\\]\nThe lower bound is the positivity in Lemma 7.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 6.1 (Uniform tree budget)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\( \\theta=1-\\frac1{p_0}<\\frac12, \\) and let \\( F(\\alpha)=D_\\alpha\\operatorname{Tr}B_{|\\alpha|}(\\alpha)^r \\) for an even moment \\(r\\) large enough for Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>Fix\n\\[\n\\theta=1-\\frac1{p_0}&lt;\\frac12,\n\\]\nand let\n\\[\nF(\\alpha)=D_\\alpha\\operatorname{Tr}B_{|\\alpha|}(\\alpha)^r\n\\]\nfor an even moment \\(r\\) large enough for Proposition 4.</p><p>Fix a root \\(\\lambda\\vdash N\\), with\n\\[\nK=k(\\lambda)&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor a fixed cutoff \\(M\\), expand a node \\(\\alpha\\vdash m\\) only when\n\\[\nm&gt;M,\n\\qquad\nk(\\alpha)&gt;m^{.01}.\n\\]\nAn expansion chooses an occurring tuple\n\\[\n(\\beta_1,\\ldots,\\beta_4)\\in I(\\alpha),\n\\qquad |\\beta_i|=m/4.\n\\]\nAll other nodes are stopped. Choose \\(r\\) sufficiently large that every stopped node has \\(F(\\alpha)\\le1\\), as allowed by Propositions 3 and 7.</p><p>Use \\(\\log0=-\\infty\\), define\n\\[\n\\log^+x=\\max(0,\\log x),\n\\]\nand, for \\(H&gt;2\\), set\n\\[\nR_H(\\lambda)\n=\n\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\lambda}\n\\log^+\\frac{\\min(i,j)}H.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(\\delta\\in(0,1/2)\\) and put\n\\[\na=\\frac12-\\delta.\n\\]\nFor every \\(H&gt;2\\) and every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), a sufficiently large fixed cutoff \\(M\\) makes the recursion satisfy\n\\[\n\\log F(\\lambda)\n\\le\n\\epsilon K\n+\n\\frac{\\theta}{a}\n\\left\\{\nR_H(\\lambda)\n+\n\\frac K2\\log\\frac NK\n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nThe cutoff and all constants in this statement are independent of the final even moment \\(r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Squaring Proposition 4 and bounding a sum by its number of terms times its largest term gives\n\\[\n\\log F(\\alpha)\n\\le\n2\\log|I(\\alpha)|\n+\n\\max_{\\boldsymbol\\beta\\in I(\\alpha)}\n\\sum_{i=1}^4\n\\{\\log F(\\beta_i)+\\theta\\log C(\\beta_i)\\}.\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\nIf \\(F(\\alpha)=0\\), no bound is needed.</p><p>Iterating (25) amounts to taking the maximum over finite four-ary trees of occurring restrictions. Each expanded node contributes its choice cost\n\\[\n2\\log|I(\\alpha)|,\n\\]\nand each of its four immediate children contributes\n\\[\n\\theta\\log C(\\beta_i).\n\\]\nThe latter charge remains present even if the child is stopped.</p><p>The tail count in Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\n\\log|I(\\alpha)|\n\\le\nC(1+\\sqrt{k(\\alpha)})\\log(2m).\n\\]\nTo apply it, orient the parent along its longest side, use the first-row inequality, and count each child’s tail with at most the parent’s deficit. Child sizes are prescribed. Thus the choice cost fits within the lower-order term of (14). Charge it at the parent, including the root.</p><p>At a level with node size \\(m\\), let \\(q_m\\) be the number of charged nodes and let their deficits be\n\\[\nk_1,\\ldots,k_{q_m}.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 6, their sum is at most \\(K\\). Expanded nodes satisfy \\(k_i&gt;m^{.01}\\), so their number is at most \\(K/m^{.01}\\). A charged child of size \\(m\\) has an expanded parent of size \\(4m\\), and there are four children per parent. Consequently, after changing one absolute constant,\n\\[\nq_m\\le CKm^{-.01},\n\\qquad\nq_m\\le N/m,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_i k_i\\le K.\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\nThe root also satisfies these bounds when expanded. A stopped root already has \\(F\\le1\\) and satisfies the conclusion directly.</p><p>At that level choose\n\\[\nh_m=\\left\\lceil m^a\\sqrt{K/N}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 5 and the choice-cost estimate, the lower-order terms are bounded by\n\\[\nC\\sum_i(1+h_m)(1+\\sqrt{k_i})\\log(2m).\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\sqrt{k_i}\\le\\sqrt{q_mK}.\n\\]\nThe terms arising from the ceiling and the constant one are therefore at most\n\\[\nCKm^{-.005}\\log(2m).\n\\]</p><p>For the terms involving \\(m^a\\), use both bounds in (27):\n\\[\nm^a\\sqrt{K/N}\\sqrt{q_mK}\n\\le Km^{-\\delta},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nm^a\\sqrt{K/N}\\,q_m\n\\le CKm^{-\\delta-.005}.\n\\]\nFor the second inequality,\n\\[\nq_m\\le\\sqrt{(N/m)(CK/m^{.01})}.\n\\]</p><p>These costs are summable geometric tails because level sizes differ by a factor four. All charged nodes have size greater than \\(M/4\\), including the stopped children of the last expanded level. Choosing \\(M\\) large makes their total at most \\(\\epsilon K\\).</p><p>It remains to sum the leading hook terms. At every level, iteration of Proposition 6 bounds the sum of \\(t_{h_m}\\) over charged nodes by \\(t_{h_m}(\\lambda)\\). Follow one root box \\((i,j)\\), and write\n\\[\ns=\\min(i,j).\n\\]\nIt can contribute only if\n\\[\ns&gt;h_m\\ge m^a\\sqrt{K/N}.\n\\]\nThe eligible sizes \\(m\\) form part of a geometric progression of ratio four, all larger than \\(M/4\\). By increasing \\(M\\) so that\n\\[\n(M/4)^a\\ge4^aH,\n\\]\ntheir number is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{\n\\log^+(s/H)+\\tfrac12\\log(N/K)\n}{\na\\log4\n}.\n\\]</p><p>Indeed, the number is bounded by the positive part of one plus the logarithm of the ratio of the upper endpoint\n\\[\n(s\\sqrt{N/K})^{1/a}\n\\]\nto the lower endpoint \\(M/4\\), divided by \\(\\log4\\); the choice of \\(M\\) absorbs the extra one. Replacing \\(\\log(s/H)\\) by its positive part can only enlarge the bound.</p><p>Boxes in the longest first row or column never contribute, since \\(h_m\\ge1\\). There are exactly \\(K\\) remaining boxes. Multiply their level counts by \\(\\theta\\log4\\), and add the lower-order costs. This proves (26).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 6.2 (Hook comparison)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Orient \\(\\lambda\\vdash N\\) so that its first row is a longest side:", "statement": "<p>Orient \\(\\lambda\\vdash N\\) so that its first row is a longest side:\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=\\max(\\lambda_1,\\lambda'_1).\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nK=N-\\lambda_1&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n\\zeta=(\\lambda_2,\\lambda_3,\\ldots)\\vdash K.\n\\]\nFor \\(H&gt;2\\), let\n\\[\nR_H(\\lambda)\n=\n\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\lambda}\n\\log^+\\frac{\\min(i,j)}H.\n\\]</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\log D_\\lambda\n\\ge\nK\\log(N/K)+\\log D_\\zeta-E_{N,K},\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nR_H(\\lambda)\\le\\log D_\\zeta+\\frac{4K}{H},\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nE_{N,K}=O(K/N)\n\\quad\\text{for }K&lt;N/3,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nE_{N,K}=O(\\sqrt N\\log(2N))\n\\quad\\text{for }K\\ge N/3.\n\\]</p><p>There is also an absolute \\(c&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\log D_\\lambda\\ge cK\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(N\\) and every nontrivial, nonsign \\(\\lambda\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The hook-length formula separates the first row exactly:\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\n=\n\\binom NKD_\\zeta\n\\prod_{j=1}^{\\lambda_1}\n\\left(\n1+\\frac{\\lambda'_j-1}{\\lambda_1-j+1}\n\\right)^{-1}.\n\\]\nIf \\(K&lt;N/3\\), a nonzero numerator occurs only for \\(j\\le K\\), so the logarithm of the inverted product is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{K}{N-2K+1}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(K\\ge N/3\\), split the columns at height \\(\\sqrt N\\). There are at most \\(\\sqrt N\\) columns taller than this; each contributes at most \\(\\log(1+N)\\). In the remaining columns, bound \\(\\log(1+x)\\) by \\(x\\) and sum the denominators harmonically. Their contribution is at most\n\\[\n\\sqrt N(1+\\log N).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\binom NK\\ge(N/K)^K,\n\\]\nthis proves (28).</p><p>Every box contributing to \\(R_H\\) lies in \\(\\zeta\\). At root coordinates \\((i,j)\\), its hook in \\(\\zeta\\) has length at most\n\\[\n\\frac K{i-1}+\\frac Kj\n\\le\n\\frac{4K}{\\min(i,j)}.\n\\]\nTake a reverse linear extension of the Young diagram order on \\(\\zeta\\), numbering its boxes from \\(1\\) to \\(K\\). The number assigned to each box is at least its hook length, because all boxes to its right and below precede it. The product of all number-to-hook ratios is exactly \\(D_\\zeta\\), and every ratio is at least one.</p><p>Suppose \\(t\\) boxes contribute to \\(R_H\\). Their distinct assigned numbers have product at least \\(t!\\). Keeping only their ratios in the hook product gives, for \\(t&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\log D_\\zeta\n\\ge\nR_H-t\\log\\frac{4eK}{tH}\n\\ge\nR_H-\\frac{4K}{H}.\n\\]\nThe last inequality follows by maximizing\n\\[\nt\\log\\frac{4eK}{tH}\n\\]\nover \\(t&gt;0\\). For \\(t=0\\), the assertion is immediate. This proves (29).</p><p>Finally, prove \\(\\log D_\\lambda\\ge cK\\) uniformly in the shape. If \\(K&lt;N/3\\), (28) already gives this for large \\(N\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nL=\\lambda_1=\\max(\\lambda_1,\\lambda'_1).\n\\]\nIf \\(K\\ge N/3\\) and \\(L\\ge N/8\\), retain the first row and an order ideal of\n\\[\nb=\\lfloor N/32\\rfloor\n\\]\nboxes in the lower diagram. Place \\(1,\\ldots,b\\) in the first \\(b\\) boxes of the top row, choose any \\(b\\) of the remaining \\(L\\) labels for the lower diagram, fill those lower boxes in a fixed standard order, and fill the rest of the top row increasingly. This gives\n\\[\n\\binom Lb\\ge4^b\n\\]\nstandard tableaux of the retained diagram. Every such tableau extends to \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>If \\(L&lt;N/8\\), every hook is shorter than \\(N/4\\), so\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\n\\ge\n\\frac{N!}{(N/4)^N}\n\\ge\n(4/e)^N.\n\\]\nThese cases give one absolute positive \\(c\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Passage from the positive moment to independent forward sweeps.** Let \\(T_N(\\lambda)\\) be the Fourier matrix of one sweep and \\( B_N(\\lambda)=T_N(\\lambda)T_N(\\lambda)^*. \\) Assume that, for fixed \\(r\\ge2\\) and …", "statement": "<p>**Passage from the positive moment to independent forward sweeps.**</p><p>Let \\(T_N(\\lambda)\\) be the Fourier matrix of one sweep and\n\\[\nB_N(\\lambda)=T_N(\\lambda)T_N(\\lambda)^*.\n\\]\nAssume that, for fixed \\(r\\ge2\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), independent of dyadic \\(N\\),\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\operatorname{Tr}B_N(\\lambda)^r\n\\le\nD_\\lambda^{\\,1-\\eta}\n\\qquad(\\lambda\\vdash N).\n\\]</p><p>Then, for every integer \\(q\\ge r\\),\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\|T_N(\\lambda)^q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n\\le\nD_\\lambda^{\\,1-\\eta q/r}.\n\\tag{31}\n\\]</p><p>If also \\(q&gt;r/\\eta\\), then\n\\[\n\\|\\mu_N^{*q}-U_{S_N}\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(N\\to\\infty),\n\\]\nuniformly over deterministic starting decks.</p>", "proof": "<p>The positive moment assumption gives\n\\[\n\\|B_N(\\lambda)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^r\n\\le\n\\operatorname{Tr}B_N(\\lambda)^r\n\\le\nD_\\lambda^{-\\eta}.\n\\]\nFor a matrix \\(A\\), use the unnormalized Schatten norm\n\\[\n\\|A\\|_p^p=\\operatorname{Tr}|A|^p.\n\\]\nFor any integer \\(q\\ge r\\), Schatten Hölder and the operator norm bound give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|T_N(\\lambda)^q\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n&amp;\\le\n\\|T_N(\\lambda)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2(q-r)}\n\\|T_N(\\lambda)\\|_{2r}^{2r}\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\|B_N(\\lambda)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^{q-r}\n\\operatorname{Tr}B_N(\\lambda)^r.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(D_\\lambda\\) and using the assumed moment estimate yields (31).</p><p>Choose an integer \\(q\\ge r\\) with \\(q&gt;r/\\eta\\). Then\n\\[\nA=\\eta q/r-1&gt;0.\n\\]\nLemma 10.1 shows that the sum of (31) over all nonsign, nontrivial representations tends to zero. The sign block is zero by Proposition 3.</p><p>The Fourier convention is\n\\[\n\\widehat\\sigma(\\lambda)\n=\n\\sum_{g\\in S_N}\\sigma(g)\\rho_\\lambda(g);\n\\]\nconvolution multiplies these matrices in the same order. Finite-group Plancherel and Cauchy–Schwarz state that any probability \\(\\sigma\\) on \\(S_N\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\|\\sigma-U_{S_N}\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}^2\n&amp;\\le\nN!\\sum_g|\\sigma(g)-1/N!|^2\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{\\lambda\\ne(N)}\nD_\\lambda\\|\\widehat\\sigma(\\lambda)\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nApply this to\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\mu_N^{*q},\n\\qquad\n\\widehat\\sigma(\\lambda)=T_N(\\lambda)^q.\n\\]\nThis proves convergence to uniform after \\(q\\) forward sweeps.</p><p>Translating the law by any deterministic starting deck preserves total variation, so the result is uniform over starting states.</p><p>No step identifies\n\\[\n(T_NT_N^*)^q\n\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\nT_N^q(T_N^*)^q.\n\\]\nThe positive moment supplies singular-value information; Hölder is the separate passage to forward evolution.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Degree summability, paper equation (30).** For every fixed \\(A>0\\), \\( \\sum_{\\substack{\\lambda\\vdash N\\\\ \\lambda\\ne(N),(1^N)}} D_\\lambda^{-A} \\longrightarrow0 \\qquad(N\\to\\infty). \\) Here \\(D_\\lambda\\) is the dimension …", "statement": "<p>**Degree summability, paper equation (30).**</p><p>For every fixed \\(A&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\lambda\\vdash N\\\\\n\\lambda\\ne(N),(1^N)}}\nD_\\lambda^{-A}\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(N\\to\\infty).\n\\tag{30}\n\\]\nHere \\(D_\\lambda\\) is the dimension of the irreducible representation of \\(S_N\\) indexed by \\(\\lambda\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>There are at most \\(2p(k)\\) partitions with\n\\[\nk(\\lambda)=N-\\max(\\lambda_1,\\lambda'_1)=k,\n\\]\nbecause deleting the longer first row or column leaves a partition of \\(k\\).</p><p>The partition generating function gives\n\\[\np(k)\\le e^{3\\sqrt{k}}.\n\\]\nIndeed, evaluate it at \\(x=e^{-1/\\sqrt{k}}\\), and bound its logarithm by\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\frac1{j(e^{j/\\sqrt{k}}-1)}\n\\le\n\\sqrt{k}\\sum_{j\\ge1}j^{-2}\n\\le2\\sqrt{k}.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 9 bounds \\(D_\\lambda^{-A}\\) by \\(e^{-Ack}\\) for all sufficiently large \\(N\\). The resulting bound\n\\[\n2e^{3\\sqrt{k}-Ack}\n\\]\nis summable in \\(k\\).</p><p>For each fixed \\(k\\ge1\\), the first-row hook identity in Proposition 9 gives\n\\[\nD_\\lambda\\to\\infty\n\\qquad(N\\to\\infty),\n\\]\nuniformly over those finitely many tails. Dominated convergence proves (30).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [0, 4, "cite"], [0, 7, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [6, 12, "cite"], [6, 16, "cite"], [6, 18, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 16, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 16, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [12, 1, "cite"], [12, 16, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 12, "cite"], [16, 1, "cite"], [17, 1, "cite"], [17, 19, "cite"], [18, 1, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"]]});
